    "available_at": "2026-11-08T12:26:38.854813+00:00",
    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Yamaha Previews the Future of the Tenere Adventure Bikes With Mysterious 4 Concept\nSuzuki-Backed Japanese eVTOL Gears Up for Commercial Service in South Korea\n8 photos\nPhoto: SkyDrive\nSkyDrive has entered a tripartite agreement with Goyang Special City and Verty to establish a comprehensive framework that will eventually support regular commercial operations for the Japanese eVTOL (electric vertical takeoff and landing) in South Korea.\nEarlier this week, during the DRONE-UAM EXPO 2026 event, held at KINTEX in Goyang, visitors had the opportunity to take a closer look at a small, 20-percent-scale mockup of the SD-05, SkyDrive’s flagship aircraft. The exhibition was co-hosted by SkyDrive and Verty, a Seoul-based company specializing in integrated systems and air traffic management solutions for vertiports.\nGoyang Special City is set to become an AAM (Advanced Air Mobility) hub. It was officially designated the primary metropolitan demonstration site for South Korea’s famous national K-UM Grand Challenge, and it’s currently working on multiple levels to advance AAM operations, from building vertiport testbeds to training future aerospace workforce in collaboration with Korea Aerospace University.\nTogether, SkyDrive, Goyang Special City, and Verty will work to establish an ecosystem for upcoming flight trials and commercial eVTOL services in Goyang. If things fall into place, commercial operations could kick off as soon as 2028.\nSkyDrive doesn’t just have an award-winning eVTOL design, but also the adequate large-scale manufacturing capabilities, thanks to its strong ties to Suzuki Motor Corporation. Two years ago, the Japanese company officially confirmed that it would begin aircraft production at a facility owned by Suzuki. In 2025, Suzuki also helped seal the deal between SkyDrive and the Gujarat-based JetSetGo Aviation Services for a huge pre-order of up to 50 SD-05 aircraft.\nThis week, Japan’s Finance Minister, Satsuki Katayama payed a special visit to SkyDrive, which included a short flight demonstration , as well as discussions of future manufacturing plans with Suzuki representatives.\nEarlier this year, SkyDrive was granted the ADO (Approved Design organization) certification from JCAB (the Japan Civil Aviation Bureau), the equivalent of the ODA (Organization Designation Authorization) from FAA in the US. With that certification in hand, SkyDrive became the first AAM company in Japan to reach this far in the type certification process.\nLike all eVTOL makers, SkyDrive is aiming for international success beyond the national borders of Japan. Right now, it’s cooperating on commercialization projects with operators and authorities in ten countries.\nThis also includes the US. This past summer, SkyDrive kicked off official meetings with FAA representatives for the type certification procedures in the US. Back in 2023, the Japanese company had set up a local subsidiary here, specifically to facilitate its official entry on the American market. In 2024, SkyDrive’s type certificate application, submitted through JCAB, was officially accepted by the FAA.\nSkyDrive’s SD-05 was purposely designed as a compact passenger eVTOL and an alternative to the heavier eVTOLs on the market. It targets short-distance urban flights of around 15 km (9 miles) at a top speed of 100 kph (62 mph).\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\njapanese evtol SkyDrive SD-05 eVTOL evtol\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1995 Acura NSX-T Had Just One Owner For a Quarter of Century, Has Less Than 57k Miles on Odometer",
